**Alcohol dependence** is when drinking becomes something you **feel you cannot manage without**. Your body gets used to alcohol and you may feel unwell when you stop. It is a **health condition**, not a weakness, and people recover every day.

## Signs of alcohol dependence

- **worrying** about where your next drink will come from
- feeling a **strong urge** to drink
- **needing more** alcohol to get the same effect
- drinking **in the morning**, or to stop shaking
- finding it **hard to stop** once you start
- carrying on drinking despite harm to your **health, work or relationships**
- **withdrawal symptoms** when you stop, such as shaking, sweating, anxiety, feeling sick or trouble sleeping

{% callout type="danger" title="Do not stop suddenly without advice" %}
If you drink heavily every day, **stopping suddenly can be dangerous** and can cause fits or a serious condition called delirium tremens. Talk to your GP or local alcohol service so you can cut down or stop **safely**. Call **999** if someone has a fit, is very confused, or is seeing things that are not there after stopping drinking.
{% /callout %}

## Why it happens

There is no single cause. Stress, trauma, mental health problems, loneliness, family history and how easy alcohol is to get can all play a part.

## Health risks

Long-term heavy drinking can lead to **liver disease**, **heart disease**, some **cancers**, **pancreatitis**, **brain damage** and **depression and anxiety**.

## Getting help

- **Your GP** can help you plan how to cut down or stop safely.
- **Local drug and alcohol services** are free and confidential, and you can usually **refer yourself**.
- **Drinkline** is a free helpline on **0300 123 1110**.

See [treatment for alcohol dependence](/conditions/mental-health/alcohol-dependence/treatment/).
